I found an acceptable family: search for an isomorphism between two succinctly encoded pentagon solutions. With the paper’s first matched-pair factor trivial, its solution is \(s_G(x,y)=(xy,y)\), so Definition 2.2 makes a pentagon-solution isomorphism exactly a group isomorphism. I’ll use odd-prime, class-2 exponent-\(p\) groups encoded by alternating tensors; this is a documented barrier case with no known polynomial-time algorithm, while a proposed basis change is checked by finite-field matrix identities.

The generator will avoid the paper’s easy constructions: it will not ask solvers to evaluate Theorem 4.1’s explicit formula, will use no extension permutations (Proposition 6.2 makes those isomorphisms explicit), and will avoid the involutive elementary-abelian regime described in Section 7.
